```

## Backend comparison on IOR (MPIIO baseline)

Use IOR-native backends as the reference, then compare `OMPFILE` against them.

Local compare in Apptainer:

```bash
apptainer exec --userns --no-privs --bind /scratch/rodrigo.freitas/io-playground \
  /scratch/rodrigo.freitas/io-playground/sifs/ompc-base_latest.sif \
  bash -lc '
    cd /scratch/rodrigo.freitas/io-playground/application/ior-fork
    SKIP_COMPILE=1 \
    IOR_COMPARE_NP=4 \
    IOR_COMPARE_REPEATS=2 \
    IOR_COMPARE_BLOCK_SIZE=4m \
    IOR_COMPARE_TRANSFER_SIZE=256k \
    IOR_COMPARE_SEGMENTS=8 \
    IOR_COMPARE_MODES=MPIIO,POSIX,OMPFILE_MPI \
    ./run_backend_compare.sh
  '
```

The compare runner prints:

- per-mode average write/read bandwidth and mean time
- speedup ratios and deltas versus IOR `MPIIO`
- raw CSV + per-run logs in `application/ior-fork/tmp/backend-compare/`

Distributed compare on Slurm (2 nodes by default):

```bash
sbatch /scratch/rodrigo.freitas/io-playground/application/ior-fork/run_sorgan_ior_backend_compare.sbatch
```

An AMD (hpcfund) variant is available as `run_amd_ior_backend_compare.sbatch`
(role-script container re-entry, `srun --mpi=pmix`, host spack MPICH
override, same summary-CSV contract; `rw`/`write` only — no read-prep pass).
Both wrappers build IOR in-container with `unset CPATH` (the repo clang's
resource headers on CPATH break the system compiler's stdint include chain)
and pass `CPPFLAGS`/`LDFLAGS` as `./configure` arguments so the libompfile
include/lib survive to `make`.

Inspect output:

```bash
sacct -j <job_id> --format=JobID,State,Elapsed,ExitCode
grep -E '\[ior-backend-cmp\]|Summary of all tests|api                 :' ior-backend-cmp-<job_id>.out
```

## The OMPFILE arm is retired

IOR runs here as the **MPI baseline** only (`MPIIO`, `POSIX`). The lane used
to have a split-role `OMPFILE_MPI` arm: one IOR app rank plus proxy ranks in
one MPI world, with IOR's own collectives pushed onto `MPI_COMM_SELF`
(`IOR_MPI_COMM_SELF=1`) so they would not wait on the proxies.

That arm is gone. Under root `AGENTS.md`, "Benchmark interface rule", a
benchmark running under the runtime calls no MPI at all: it expresses its
work as target regions issuing `omp_file_*` and takes any coordination from
MPP primitives. The `MPI_COMM_SELF` construction was the opposite — the
benchmark reading `LIBOMPFILE_MPP_*`, raising its own MPI thread level and
dodging the proxy ranks. It also measured the wrong thing: every byte was
issued from the single app rank, so the number was the app-rank transport,
not the I/O path.

The runtime-side IOR workload is `testior_target.cc` in
`application/hacc-io-fork`, which replicates IOR's `-F`, shared-file and
`-C`/reorderTasks access geometry with each writer inside
`#pragma omp target device(d)`. It is what both published IOR rows in
`docs/benchmark-results.md` were measured with.

`src/aiori-ompfile.c` is kept: it calls no MPI and is the natural starting
point if IOR itself is ever ported to issue its writers as target regions.
